WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.740
Hey, welcome to Planetarian Daily, episode 124.

00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.460
It's July 18th, 2026.

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:10.180
Here's what's new in science, health, and longevity research.

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:14.620
South African genomics projects now direct 10% of budgets

00:00:14.620 --> 00:00:17.760
to community-chosen benefits through local decision making.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:20.760
Researchers have used artificial intelligence

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.380
to create new SIN-TNPB enzymes that expand

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:26.180
CRISPR gene editing options.

00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:29.480
The work appears in a paper published in science.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.940
These synthetic enzymes outperform the natural reference TNPB enzyme

00:00:34.940 --> 00:00:36.660
in direct comparison tests.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:40.000
Traditional CRISPR systems depend on tools

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.000
that evolve naturally over time.

00:00:42.660 --> 00:00:45.540
The design effort focuses on producing gene editors

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:49.860
that carry properties absent from proteins found in existing systems.

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:54.600
A few episodes back the show examined gene editing safety questions.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.280
Today's work adds new enzyme options that could support

00:00:58.280 --> 00:00:59.820
in vivo applications.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.740
Performance metrics still needed before SIN-TNPB enzymes advanced

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:09.540
to mammalian testing include editing efficiency across diverse cell types,

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.480
off-target rates in complex genomes,

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:15.160
delivery compatibility with existing vectors,

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:17.900
and stability under physiological conditions.

00:01:18.820 --> 00:01:21.600
Additional benchmarks involve toxicity profiles

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:23.240
in primary human cells,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:25.360
scalability of protein production,

00:01:25.680 --> 00:01:28.840
and retention of activity after repeated dosing cycles.

00:01:29.620 --> 00:01:31.920
Researchers will also track how the enzymes

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.120
interact with chromatin states that differ from those

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.780
in bacterial or simple eukaryotic models.

00:01:38.420 --> 00:01:42.020
These data points will clarify whether the AI-designed variants

00:01:42.020 --> 00:01:44.740
can move beyond proof-of-concept assays.

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:49.280
Now, shifting to community-led frameworks in genomics research.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.560
The approach translates ethical principles into concrete action

00:01:53.560 --> 00:01:55.720
using community-led processes.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:02:03.280
Gony and Guari from the Sam RC-WELT's Rural Public Health and Health Transitions Research Unit

00:02:03.280 --> 00:02:07.260
presented the blueprint at the World Congress of Bioethics.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:12.060
Researchers, universities, funders, and industry partners can now apply

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:14.080
the operational model in practice.

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:17.880
The framework shows how to embed benefit sharing directly

00:02:17.880 --> 00:02:20.240
into ongoing genomics studies.

00:02:20.740 --> 00:02:23.620
Researchers at Coach University School of Nursing

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:28.520
adapted the artificial intelligence self-efficacy scale into Turkish.

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:33.820
Associate Professor Remzie Samrchi Shahin and Assistant Professor

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:36.760
Seida Gune led the psychometric evaluation.

00:02:37.460 --> 00:02:41.680
The study appeared in the International Journal of Human Computer Interaction.

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:47.140
The tool assesses perceived confidence in using artificial intelligence technologies

00:02:47.140 --> 00:02:48.080
among students.

00:02:48.900 --> 00:02:54.240
The adaptation process required validation steps specific to nursing student populations.

00:02:55.040 --> 00:03:00.260
Researchers examined which local mosquito species transmit the plasmodium parasite

00:03:00.260 --> 00:03:03.000
that causes avian malaria in wild birds.

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:09.040
The work focused on several UK mosquito species now identified as potential carriers.

00:03:09.860 --> 00:03:15.360
Limited prior data existed on mosquito vectors for bird diseases compared with human pathogens.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.880
The study clarifies transmission routes in UK ecosystems.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:25.100
Field collections and laboratory assays supported the species identifications.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:31.420
A University of Florida sport management study tracked participation histories of MLB players.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:37.320
Researchers found that high school track experience provides an overlooked edge in skill development.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:43.000
The analysis suggests teams may miss competitive advantages in player selection.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.320
Findings question traditional pathways into professional baseball.

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:53.380
Track training develops speed, coordination, and endurance metrics valued in scouting reports.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:58.040
Professor Janestra Bianconi at Queen Mary University of London

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.220
proposes a new framework for structure formation.

00:04:01.840 --> 00:04:06.000
Gravity from entropy theory links thermodynamics to cosmic structure.

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:12.720
The model addresses how increasing complexity can occur while obeying the second law of thermodynamics.

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:17.920
It connects entropy principles with the emergence of ordered cosmic features.

00:04:18.140 --> 00:04:22.360
The perspective offers a fresh angle on a core physics question.

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:28.640
A sole national university team led by professor's NAMQ Park and SunQU

00:04:28.640 --> 00:04:31.360
developed the photonic integrated circuit.

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:35.580
The optical chip design allows real-time light speed control.

00:04:36.280 --> 00:04:40.600
The device works with professor Xian Ji Piao at the University of Seoul.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:44.240
Simulations show the circuit can slow light on demand.

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:49.020
The advanced targets precise manipulation within photonic systems.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:54.320
CERN's LHC has stopped operations after years of proton collisions.

00:04:55.060 --> 00:04:59.540
The large Hadron collider enters long shutdown for Higgs boson upgrades.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:05.280
Work proceeds beneath the French Swiss border on the world's largest scientific instrument.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:09.600
The upgrade aims to unlock further details of the Higgs boson.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:14.160
The shutdown follows extensive data collection at near-light speeds.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:20.380
A review in liminology and oceanography led by UC San Diego's Scripps Institution

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:25.100
of Oceanography examined oxygen decline across multiple ecosystems.

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:30.700
Rapid deoxygenation affects oceans and other aquatic environments worldwide.

00:05:31.480 --> 00:05:36.500
Consequences may prove irreversible on human time scales according to the analysis.

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:41.680
The work links these changes to broader risks for Earth system stability.

00:05:42.360 --> 00:05:46.100
Wild snap dragons fine-tune petal shades to attract bees.

00:05:46.760 --> 00:05:51.120
Flowers adjust subtle color blends to gain competitive advantage in pollination.

00:05:51.760 --> 00:05:56.260
Slight shade differences can determine gene survival in the contest for bee visits.

00:05:57.060 --> 00:06:01.180
The study shows precise visual tuning beyond simple color categories.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:05.640
Researchers documented the effort plants expend on these adjustments.

00:06:05.640 --> 00:06:12.260
Alberto Borges at the University of Liege compared microbial methane oxidation rates in

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:18.240
Belgian and African rivers. Certain bacteria oxidize the gas before it reaches the atmosphere.

00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:23.680
African rivers displayed higher oxidation activity than the Belgian sites examined.

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:30.560
The biological filter remains insufficient to counter expected increases from warming and nitrate pollution.

00:06:31.520 --> 00:06:36.780
Researchers traced the 2015 equatorial fires to identify starting conditions.

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:42.780
Most large Southeast Asian fires began at several points rather than single locations.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:47.740
Ecological, climatic, and human factors were linked to ignition sites.

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:52.180
The findings aimed to improve prevention strategies under climate change.

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:57.340
Multiple origin points drive most large fires according to the spatial analysis.

00:06:58.220 --> 00:07:03.460
Thai researchers bonded silk fabric between natural rubber sheets using resource and all

00:07:03.460 --> 00:07:09.940
formaldehyde latex coating. Laboratory and pilot scale tests compared the silk composite against

00:07:09.940 --> 00:07:15.960
conventional nylon. The prototype bicycle tire replaces nylon with silk for better performance.

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:19.420
The silk version showed gains in strength and endurance.

00:07:20.120 --> 00:07:23.040
Full tires were manufactured to validate the formulation.

00:07:23.680 --> 00:07:28.740
Researchers produced a modified silk from silkworm cocoons that resist shrinkage

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:34.700
and shape loss after repeated wetting. Conventional silk loses form with moisture exposure

00:07:34.700 --> 00:07:40.900
despite its strength and biocompatibility. The new variant remains lightweight and suitable for

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:47.000
clothing and medical uses. The improvement addresses a long-standing limitation in natural silk

00:07:47.000 --> 00:07:53.460
performance. Current tools struggle to resolve specific molecules at high detail within cellular

00:07:53.460 --> 00:08:00.480
environments. Researchers created designed proteins to address this limitation. AI designed

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:06.920
proteins improve imaging of molecules inside living cells. The proteins help target and visualize

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:13.260
chosen molecular residents. The approach expands options for studying dense cellular systems.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:19.060
Most people picture the second law of thermodynamics as a slow slide into total disorder

00:08:19.060 --> 00:08:26.100
where nothing new can form. In reality, local pockets of order routinely emerge even as overall

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:32.340
entropy rises because energy flows through open systems. Right now, as you sit listening,

00:08:32.860 --> 00:08:38.660
gravitational collapse in distant gas clouds is converting potential energy into structured stars

00:08:38.660 --> 00:08:45.620
and galaxies, while radiating waste heat outward. One memorable number is that the observable universe

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:52.880
contains roughly 10 to the 80th atoms, yet has produced highly-ordered structures across 13.8

00:08:52.880 --> 00:08:58.560
billion years without violating the law. The practical takeaway is to watch for future papers

00:08:58.560 --> 00:09:05.560
that quantify how much entropy export is required for any given level of biological or cosmic complexity.

00:09:06.200 --> 00:09:12.200
Before we go, keep an eye on further tests that compare the new CIN-TNPB enzymes against

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:18.000
additional natural variants in mammalian cell models. That's Planetarian Daily for today.

